WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.780
I got an email from an
aerobatics team that said,

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.189
"Hi, we attach pyrotechnics to our planes

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:09.260
"so we can produce a fireworks show in mid-flight.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.200
"Do you want to come for a test ride?"

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.639
[radio] "Going right, now."

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.640
What else was I going to say?

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.430
Look at that sunset.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.020
Oh, wow.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.550
- My inspiration for Aerosparx came from seeing
New Years Eve on television,

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.270
in Edinburgh, where underneath the castle
they had a waterfall of light.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:36.220
I always imagined that being somehow trailing
off behind the wing of an aeroplane.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.210
- Aerosparx, as a display team, we are lucky
enough and privileged enough

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.900
to go all over the world.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.690
We've been to China, Saudi Arabia, Portugal,

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.000
lots of locations in Europe, and obviously
locations in the UK.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.940
And we can display from up to 300,000 people,
to a million people.

00:00:54.949 --> 00:00:56.050
- All right.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.540
It is nearly dark enough and our planes are
nearly in position.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.560
This is going to happen very soon.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.000
Just to be clear, this is a practice run.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.000
This is not a proper display.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.170
When it's a display,
there is a lot more going on in the cockpit,

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.939
a lot more going on the pyrotechnics,

00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:12.469
and they are very much not allowed
to take passengers.

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:15.440
But, um, yeah.
Let's see what this is like.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.480
- And, relax(!)

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.840
- Are my nerves that obvious?

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.400
- One of the big wows is flying
in extremely close formation.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.640
We're helped by the wings
being extremely long,

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:30.789
so your peripheral vision is just full of
aeroplane.

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:32.619
- I don't see Guy very often,

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:37.750
but I know that he's probably only three metres
from my aircraft,

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:38.849
and that's where trust comes in.

00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:44.099
- You'll see us firing lots of different kinds
of fireworks, pyro, from the aeroplanes.

00:01:44.099 --> 00:01:47.219
They all need preparation
and that can take some hours.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:51.590
So all of the igniters, eMatches, need inserting
into the fireworks.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.840
We need to work out what we're going to put
on the aeroplane where,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.560
and then the process of actually fitting the
aeroplane, again,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.169
can take half an hour to an hour.

00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:03.350
We genuinely can't shimmy down the wing and
light the blue to uch paper.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.439
All commercial firework displays are ignited
the same way.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:11.330
They're all ignited with a control box lighting
an eMatch or an igniter.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.780
And that goes into the blue touch paper, effectively.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.780
And we do just the same.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.810
We started out by having a bank of switches.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.150
But when you're under
high workload in formation flying,

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.630
it's so easy to hit the wrong button.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.930
We can fire 24 separate fireworks in our display.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.849
You can't get 24 right in a sequence.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.540
So we've actually had a
bespoke computer system designed,

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.670
and not only does it fire our
fireworks for us, but it also

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.790
controls the LED light system
on the aeroplanes  as well.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.180
And that means I now have only one button.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.060
So once I've pressed it once, it then auto-sequences
onto the next part of the programmes.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.780
My imagination, I've found over the years,

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:53.280
has always been a leapfrog ahead
of the authorities' regulation.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.730
Not trying to stop what we're doing,
but trying to understand what we're doing,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.380
and finding out where it fits into regulations
that were written, quite genuinely,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.960
not for aeroplanes.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.000
So at the moment we're dealing with
aviation regulation and firework regulation.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.540
[radio] Diving now.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.100
Loop now.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.820
Fire, fire, fire.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.300
Loop. Loop, now.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.220
- Check your CB, Rob,
check your CB.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.099
- So the question is, how can they do this?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.900
And how can they do this safely?

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:36.540
[radio] Loop.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.860
Loop, now.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.560
- We have defined roles.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:46.180
My job is to lead the formation,
lead the display.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.160
- Rob is working very hard in the climb out,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.609
working out exactly where
to start the display,

00:03:50.609 --> 00:03:53.609
and how to modify the display subtly

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:56.660
to keep it in the right place
with the wind conditions.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.400
My job is very clear,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.320
to fly safely and as close as I can
without hitting him.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.600
Planning is everything in our display,

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.819
and that planning starts on the ground
before we even get airborne.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:12.060
We do a bumblebee dance, as we call it,
so we go around in circles,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.380
in our mind and physically mimicking the manoeuvres
that we're then going to do in the sky.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:23.150
And that then sets a cadence and a rhythm,
and the physical memory of what you're doing,

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.670
so that when we start a display,
we're on the same page and working as a team.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:31.560
On the radio, we're confirming all the time
what we're going to do before we do it.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.660
- Okay. Firing now.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.380
- Our choice of using a Grob 109
motorglider was for lots of reasons.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.640
One of my original imaginations
of this firework display

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.420
was to separate the fireworks as far as we could,
and we needed a big wingspan for that.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.630
Because it's a glider, it also has
the efficiency of a glider.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:56.760
Which means that we can perform aerobatics
with a very small engine, and it gives us grace.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.140
The disadvantage with a motorglider is a very
small engine.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.630
That means we take a long time
to climb to altitude,

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.840
and when we're at altitude,

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.130
we try and conserve all the energy we have.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.920
We describe our as play as painting with light.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.760
We like to think that the focus is more on
the image we're painting with the aeroplanes,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.720
rather than the distinct manoeuvres we're
doing separately.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.950
Fireworks are great on the ground,

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.190
when you're flying next to fireworks,
it's even better.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:26.310
And I still pinch myself every time I even
think about going flying with fireworks,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:30.380
and just think, 'I can't believe we're actually
going to do this'.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.380
- That's incredible.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.630
So that's, what, two tests now.
And what are you doing tomorrow?

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:40.060
- Tomorrow will be twice as much pyro
in the same length of time.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.940
So it's going to be Armageddon.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.479
- Thank you so, so, much.

00:05:45.479 --> 00:05:46.949
- Very good.

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:50.910
- You can find out more about Aerosparx and
all the displays they're going to,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.650
at the link in the description.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.920
- Thank you.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.080
What we are going to do
is turn the engine off.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.700
- Oh.
- And land as a glider.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.360
- Okay. Um.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.380
Sure!

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.400
- There you are, look. No engine.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.680
The propeller's just... just stopped.

